  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What makes a grid smart?",
      "a": "Sensors, smart meters and computers share live information about supply and demand, so the grid can adjust automatically. Instead of only changing supply, it can also nudge demand to match.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a smart grid?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does a smart meter do?",
      "a": "It measures how much electricity and gas you use, usually every half hour, and sends the readings automatically to your supplier. It also lets you see your use and cost on a display at home.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a smart meter work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How can electric cars help the grid?",
      "a": "Cars can charge when power is cheap and plentiful, like overnight or on windy days. With vehicle-to-grid chargers, some can even send power back during evening peaks.",
      "asks": [
        "What is vehicle-to-grid?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is demand response?",
      "a": "Demand response means using less electricity, or using it at a different time, when the grid is stretched. Factories, heat pumps and car chargers can be paid to pause briefly, which can be cheaper than running an extra power station.",
      "asks": [
        "What is demand side response?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a time-of-use tariff?",
      "a": "It is an electricity price that changes during the day: cheaper at night or when renewables are plentiful, dearer at the evening peak. It encourages people to run washing machines or charge cars at quiet times.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does electricity cost more at some times?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Smart meters in Britain usually record readings every half hour.",
    "Vehicle-to-grid technology lets suitably equipped electric cars send power back to the grid."
  ],
